- At low pH values, methylene blue is more positively charged, which can enhance its interaction with the negatively charged surface of activated carbon, leading to higher removal efficiency.
- At high pH values, methylene blue is less charged, which can result in lower removal efficiency.
- Higher temperatures can increase the rate of adsorption, leading to faster removal of methylene blue.
- Longer contact times can result in higher removal efficiency, as more methylene blue molecules have a a longer time to interact with the activated carbon surface.

In summary, the percentage removal of methylene blue from a water sample using activated carbon as an adsorbent depends on various factors such as pH, temperature, and contact time. To determine the exact percentage removal, you would need to perform an experiment and analyze the data.
