ΔHhydr(H2O) = -41 kJ/mol (since water is a product)
ΔHhydr(Fe2+) = -340 kJ/mol
ΔHhydr(Cl-) = -381 kJ/mol

Now, we can plug these values into the equation:

ΔH = (-392 kJ/mol) + 6(-41 kJ/mol) - [(-340 kJ/mol) + 2(-381 kJ/mol)]

ΔH = -392 kJ/mol - 246 kJ/mol + 340 kJ/mol + 762 kJ/mol

ΔH = 584 kJ/mol

The standard enthalpy change for the reaction between [Fe(H2o)6]2+ and 2Cl- ions to produce [FeCl4]2- complex ion is 584 kJ/mol.
