","In a double-stranded DNA molecule, the number of adenine (A) pairs to thymine (T), and the number of guanine (G) pairs to cytosine (C). Therefore, the total number of each base in the double-stranded molecule is twice the number on the single strand.

- Adenine (A): 20 (on the single strand) × 2 = 40 in the double-stranded molecule.
- Guanine (G): 25 (on the single strand) × 2 = 50 in the double-stranded molecule.
- Cytosine (C): 30 (on the single strand) × 2 = 60 in the double-stranded molecule.
- Thymine (T): 22 (on the single strand) × 2 = 44 in the double-stranded molecule.

So, the complete double-stranded DNA molecule contains 40 A, 50 G, 60 C, and 44 T.
