What is the difference between isotopologues and isotopomers?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","Isotopologues and isotopomers are two closely related terms in chemistry that describe variations in the chemical structure due to isotopic substitution. The key difference lies in whether the substitution affects the molecular structure or just the chemical shift in NMR spectroscopy.

1. **Isotopologues**: Isotopologues are molecules that have the same molecular formula but differ in the isotopic composition of their atoms. For example, \( \text{C}_6\text{H}_2\text{O}_3 \) can be represented by \( ^1\text{C}_6\text{H}_2\text{O}_3 \) (normal carbon) and \( ^2\text{C}_6\text{H}_2\text{O}_3 \) (carbon-13). These are two isotopologues of the same molecule.

2. **Isotomers**: Isotomers are molecules that have the same molecular formula and the same connectivity of atoms but differ in the isotopic composition of their atoms. For example, \( \text{C}_6\text{H}_2\text{O}_3 \) can be represented by \( ^1\text{C}_6\text{H}_2\text{O}_3 \) (normal carbon) and \( ^2\text{C}_6\text{H}_2\text{O}_3 \) (carbon-19). These are two isotomers of the same molecule.

In summary, the key difference is that isotopologues are defined based on the isotopic composition of the atoms, while isotomers are defined based on the isotopic composition of the atoms and the connectivity of the atoms.
