When Grignard reagents react with carbon dioxide (CO₂), they primarily produce carboxylic acids. The reaction proceeds as follows:
\[ \text{R-Mg-X} + \text{CO}_2 \rightarrow \text{R-COOH} + \text{Mg(OH)X} \]
This is because CO₂ acts as an electrophile, and the Grignard reagent (a strong nucleophile) attacks the carbon of CO₂, forming a carboxylate intermediate that is protonated to yield the carboxylic acid. 

Thus, the correct answer is $\boxed{\text{(a) CO}_2}$, as it leads to the formation of a carboxylic acid, a type of carbonyl compound. The other options produce different carbonyl compounds (e.g., ketones or aldehydes), but CO₂ is the most straightforward and common reagent for this transformation.
