","When solid benzoic acid (H(C₇H₅O₂)) reacts with sodium hydroxide (NaOH), the following observable effects occur:  
1. The solid benzoic acid dissolves in the aqueous sodium hydroxide solution.  
2. The solution becomes basic (alkaline) due to the formation of hydroxide ions (OH⁻).  
3. The solution may also be warmed due to the heat released during the reaction.  

The fully ionized equation for the reaction is:  
\[
\text{H(C}_7\text{H}_5\text{O}_2 (s)} + \text{NaOH (aq)} \rightarrow \text{Na(C}_7\text{H}_5\text{O}_2 (aq)} + \text{H}_2\text{O (l)}
\]  
This ionic equation shows the dissociation of benzoic acid (H(C₇H₅O₂)) and sodium hydroxide (NaOH) into their respective ions, and the formation of sodium benzoate (Na(C₇H₅O₂)) and water (H₂O).  

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
&\text{H(C}_7\text{H}_5\text{O}_2 (s)} + \text{Na}^+ (aq) + \text{OH}^- (aq) \rightarrow \text{Na}^+ (aq) + \text{C}_7\text{H}_5\text{O}_2^- (aq) + \text{H}_2\text{O (l)} \\
&\text{H(C}_7\text{H}_5\text{O}_2 (s)} + \text{OH}^- (aq) \rightarrow \text{C}_7\text{H}_5\text{O}_2^- (aq) + \text{H}_2\text{O (l)}
\end{aligned}
}
