","The key difference between σ (sigma) and π (pi) molecular orbitals lies in the electron density distribution:  
- **σ molecular orbitals**: The electron density is concentrated along the axis between the nuclei of the bonded atoms. This is because the atomic orbitals (e.g., 2s or 2p) overlap head-to-to-off, resulting in a symmetrical electron density distribution.  
- **π molecular orbitals**: The electron density is concentrated above and below the plane joining the nuclei of the bonded atoms. This occurs because the atomic orbitals overlap side-by-side, resulting in a directional electron density distribution.  

Thus, σ orbitals are formed by head-on overlap, while π orbitals are formed by side-by-side overlap.  

$\boxed{\text{σ: electron density along the axis between nuclei; π: electron density above/below the plane between nuclei}}$
