A jogger running at 9 km/hr alongside a railway track is 150 m ahead of the engine of a train running at 45 km/hr in the same direction. The train takes 25 seconds to pass the jogger. How long is the train in meters?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","1. **Convert the speed of the train to meters per second**:  
   \( 45 \text{ km/hr} \times \frac{1000 \text{ m}}{1 \text{ km}} \times \frac{1 \text{ hr}}{60 \text{ hr}} = 12 \text{ m/s} \).

2. **Calculate the relative speed of the train relative to the jogger**:  
   \( 12 \text{ m/s} - 9 \text{ m/s} = 3 \text{ m/s} \).  
   The train moves 3 m/s faster than the jogger.

3. **Calculate the total distance the train covers to pass the jogger**:  
   The distance is the sum of the jogger's initial distance (150 m) and the length of the train. Let the length of the train be \( x \) meters.  
   Total distance = \( 150 + x \) meters.

4. **Use the time to find the length of the train**:  
   Time = Distance / Speed  
   \( 25 \text{ s} = \frac{150 + x}{3 \text{ m/s}} \).  
   Solving for \( x \):  
   \( 75 = 150 + x \).  
   \( x = 150 - 75 = 75 \text{ m} \).

   The length of the train is \(\boxed{75 \text{ m}}\).
