","To prove this, we use the definition of a polyhedral cone and the properties of linear hyperplanes.

1. **Existence of the Hyperplane**:
   - The cone \( C \) is defined as the set of all linear combinations of \( a_1, \ldots, a_n \) with nonnegative coefficients.
   - The set \( \text{span}(a_1, \ldots, a_n)\} \) is the set of all linear combinations of \( a_1, \ldots, a_n \), which spans the span of the vectors \( a_1, \ldots, a_n \).
   - If \( b \) is not in \( C \), then \( b \) is not a linear combination of \( a_1, \ldots, a_n \) with nonnegative coefficients.
   - The span of \( a_1, \ldots, a_n \) is a subspace of \( \mathbb{R}^n \), and \( b \) is not in this subspace.
   - By the Fundamental Lemma of the Simple Method, there exists a hyperplane \( H \) such that \( \text{span}(a_1, \ldots, a_n) \) lies on one side of \( H \) and \( b \) lies on the other side.

2. **Properties of \( H \)**:
   - The hyperplane \( H \) is tangent to \( C \) at the origin, so \( H \) is orthogonal to the direction of the cone.
   - The normal vector of \( H \) is a nonzero linear form \( y \in (\mathbb{R}^n)^* \).
   - The condition \( y a_i \geq 0 \) for all \( i = 1, \ldots, n \) implies that \( y \) is a nonnegative linear form in the direction of the cone.
   - The condition \( y b < 0 \) implies that \( b \) lies on the opposite side of \( H \) compared to \( C \).

3. **Conclusion**:
   - The existence of such a hyperplane \( H \) and a corresponding nonzero linear form \( y \) satisfies the given conditions.
   - This ensures that \( C \) lies entirely on one side of \( H \), while \( b \) lies on the other side, confirming the requirement that \( C \) and \( b \) are on opposite sides of the hyperplane.

Thus, we have shown that for any point \( b \) outside a polyhedral cone \( C \), there exists a hyperplane \( H \) and a nonzero linear form \( y \) that satisfy the given conditions.
