","The recurrence relation for the number of comparisons in QuickScope in the best-case scenario is:
\[
C(n) = \begin{cases}
0 & \text{if } n = 1, \\
C\left\lfloor \frac{n}{2} \right\rfloor + C\left\lceil \frac{n}{2} \right\rfloor + n - 1 & \text{if } n > 1.
\end{cases}
\]
This recurrence is derived as follows:
1. **Base Case (n = 1):** If the input list has only one element, there are no comparisons needed, so \( C(1) = 0 \).
2. **General Case (n > 1):**
   - The first comparison is to compare the pivot event with the first element of the sublist.
   - The first comparison splits the input of size \( n \) into two equal-sized sublike, each of size \(\left\lfloor \frac{n}{2} \right\rfloor \) and \(\left\lceil \frac{n}{2} \right\rfloor\).
   - The number of comparisons in the two sublike is \( C\left\lfloor \frac{n}{2} \right\rfloor \) and \( C\left\lceil \frac{n}{2} \right\rfloor \), respectively.
   - The total number of comparisons is the sum of these two sums plus the one comparison for the pivot element. Thus, \( C(n) = C\left\lfloor \frac{n}{2} \right\rfloor + C\left\lceil \frac{n}{2} \right\rfloor + n - 1 \).

The term \( n - 1 \) accounts for the remaining comparisons, which are performed to compare the pivot element with the elements in its subleaves. 

**Final Answer:**
\[
C(n) = \begin{cases}
0 & \text{if } n = 1, \\
C\left\lfloor \frac{n}{2} \right\rfloor + C\left\lceil \frac{n}{2} \right\rceil + n - 1 & \text{if } n > 1.
\end{cases}
\]
