","The magnetic field strength at the center of a circular coil is given by the formula:
\[
B = \frac{\mu_0 n I}{2R}
\]
where:
- \( B \) is the magnetic field strength,
- \(\mu_0 = 4\pi \times 10^{-7} \, \text{T} \cdot \text{m/A}\) is the permeability of free space,
- \( n \) is the number of turns in the coil,
- \( I \) is the current flowing through the coil,
- \( R \) is the radius of the coil.

Substituting the given values:
\[
B = \frac{(4\pi \times 10^{-7} \, \text{T} \cdot \text{m/A})(100)(1 \, \text{A})}{2 \times 0.2 \, \text{m}}
\]
\[
B = \frac{4\pi \times 10^{-5} \, \text{T} \cdot \text{m}}{0.4 \, \text{m}}
\]
\[
B = \pi \times 10^{-5} \, \text{T}
\]
\[
B \approx 3.14 \times 10^{-5} \, \text{T}
\]

Thus, the magnetic field strength at the center of the coil is \(\boxed{3.14 \times 10^{-5} \, \text{T}}\).
