<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
(a) The force on the proton is given by \(F = qE\), where \(q\) is the charge of the proton and \(E\) is the electric field strength.  
\[
F = (1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C}) \times (500 \, \text{N/C}) = 8.00 \times 10^{-17} \, \text{N}
\]
The acceleration \(a\) is then:  
\[
a = \frac{F}{m} = \frac{8.00 \times 10^{-17} \, \text{N}}{1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}} = 4.75 \times 10^9 \, \text{m/s}^2
\]
\[
\boxed{4.75 \times 10^9 \, \text{m/s}^2}
\]

(b) The time \(t\) can be calculated using the kinematic equation:  
\[
v = u + at
\]
where \(u = 0\) (initial speed), \(v = 35,000 \, \text{m/s}\), and \(a = 4.75 \times 10^9 \, \text{m/s}^2\).  
\[
t = \frac{v - u}{a} = \frac{35,000 \, \text{m/s}}{4.75 \times 10^9 \, \text{m/s}^2} = 7.00 \times 10^{-5} \, \text{s}
\]
\[
\boxed{7.00 \times 10^{-5} \, \text{s}}
\]

