The curl of a vector field \(\mathbf{V}(x, y)\) in Cartesian coordinates is given by:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \left( \frac{\partial z}{\partial y} - \frac{\partial w}{\partial z} \right) \mathbf{i} + \left( \frac{\partial w}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial z}{\partial y} \right) \mathbf{j} + \left( \frac{\partial y}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial x}{\partial y} \right) \mathbf{k}.
\]
If \(\mathbf{V}(x, y)\) depends only on \(x\) and \(y\), then \(z = w = 0\). Substituting these into the curl equation, we get:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = 0 \mathbf{i} + 0 \mathbf{j} + 0 \mathbf{k}.
\]
Thus, the curl of \(\mathbf{V}(x, y)\) is \(\boxed{0}\).

