   \]  
   where \(Pr\) is the Prandtl number. For air at 500°C, \(Pr \approx 0.0001\). Substituting the values:  
   \[
   Nu = 0.004 \times 40,000^{0.25} \times 0.0001^{0.7} \approx 0.004
   \]  
   The heat transfer coefficient \(h\) is:  
   \[
   h = \frac{Nu \cdot k}{\delta}
   \]  
   where \(k\) is the thermal conductivity of air at 500°C and \(\delta\) is the characteristic length (in this case, the plate length). For air at 500°C, \(k \approx 0.005 \, \text{W/m·K}\).  
   \[
   h = \frac{0.004 \times 0.005}{0.2} \approx 0.001 \, \text{W/m}^2 \cdot \text{K}
   \]  
   The rate of heat transfer \(Q\) is:  
   \[
   Q = h \cdot A \cdot \Delta T
   \]  
   where \(A = 0.2 \times 0.1 = 0.02 \, \text{m}^2\) and \(\Delta T = 500 - 100 = 400 \, \text{K}\).  
   \[
   Q = 0.001 \times 0.02 \times 400 = 0.008 \, \text{W}
   \]  

2. **Percentage Change in Heat Transfer:**  
   When the velocity is doubled and the pressure is increased to 5 atm, the Reynolds number increases by a factor of 2, and the pressure effect is negligible for this flow regime. The new Reynolds number is \(80,000\), leading to:  
   \[
   Nu = 0.004 \times 80,000^{0.25} \times 0.0001^{0.7} \approx 0.005
   \]  
